import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "Email",
	description: "packages/email, a plugin for Roc Solid's server: email over SMTP, from a job so a server that doesn't answer is tried again; or kept in memory for the dev mailbox while developing.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "send", label: "Sending one" },
		{ id: "smtp", label: "Over SMTP" },
		{ id: "mailbox", label: "The dev mailbox" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The ecosystem · email"
				title="Email that goes out over SMTP, or waits in a mailbox while you develop"
			>
				A welcome, a sign-in link, a receipt: <code>Email.send!</code> hands a message to an SMTP server, as
				text or text and HTML. Without one, it keeps the message in the server's memory, and the dev mailbox
				shows it, so development sends nothing.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				Email is a package, <code>packages/email</code>, and a plugin (D-035): Roc (<code>Email.roc</code>, an
				SMTP client in <code>Smtp.roc</code>, the mailbox in <code>Mailbox.roc</code>) on what the server
				platform gives any plugin: sockets with TLS, values in memory, and paths answered before the app
				under <code>--dev</code> (<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugins</a>). Its pages are in
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_admin}>the admin's</a> look.
			</p>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.email_plugin.file}, in part"} source={Sources.email_plugin.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The app names the package in its header (<code>{"email: \"…/packages/email/main.roc\""}</code>) and lists
				its plugin: <code>{"Email.plugin({ from, smtp_url! })"}</code>. Both have defaults: <code>from</code>,
				who mail is from, is <code>MAIL_FROM</code> from the environment (or <code>rocx &lt;rocx@localhost&gt;</code>);
				<code>smtp_url!</code>, where it goes, reads <code>SMTP_URL</code> when the server starts, unless the app
				gives a function that finds it elsewhere (a secret kept in a vault). The plugin adds the dev mailbox's
				paths and a look at the SMTP server.
			</p>

			<Heading id="send">Sending one</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_mail_job.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_mail_job.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Email.send!({ to, subject, text, html })"}</code> waits for the SMTP server's answer: <code>Ok</code>,
				or <code>EmailErr</code> with why (an address it can't read, a server that refuses or isn't there). Send
				from a job (<a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>packages/jobs</a>): one that fails is sent again later, and the
				request that queued it doesn't wait.
			</p>

			<Heading id="smtp">Over SMTP</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Where:</strong> <code>smtp://user:password@host:587?tls=required</code> (STARTTLS), or
					<code>smtps://…:465</code> (TLS from the start), and <code>AUTH PLAIN</code> when the URL has a user.
					The SMTP client is Roc, over the platform's <code>Tcp</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What:</strong> text, or text and HTML (<code>multipart/alternative</code>); a subject that
					isn't ASCII is encoded, a body that isn't is sent as base64.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>At start</strong> the plugin connects to the SMTP server, TLS and all, and logs whether it
					answers (<code>email.smtp</code>): a wrong URL shows then, not at the first email. A server that
					doesn't answer doesn't stop the app.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="mailbox">The dev mailbox</Heading>
			<p>
				A server started with <code>--dev</code> (as <code>just dev</code> starts it) answers
				<code>/__rocx/mail</code> before the app: the email it kept rather than sent, newest first (when, from,
				to, subject), with a Clear button, and each one at <code>/__rocx/mail/&lt;id&gt;</code>: its headers,
				its text, its HTML in a sandboxed frame (no script runs in it; a link opens a new tab), and the message
				whole, as SMTP would have carried it. The list updates as mail comes. The last 100 are kept in the
				server's memory (<a href={"${Paths.platform_live}#kv"}><code>Kv</code></a>), so a restart forgets them.
				<code>/__rocx/mail.json</code> is the same as JSON, for tests (<a href={Paths.ecosystem_testing}>packages/testing</a>
				reads it): with <code>--debug</code> alone, only the JSON is served. Without <code>--dev</code> or
				<code>--debug</code>, those paths are the app's.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>No attachments, Cc or Bcc.</li>
				<li>Mail kept for the mailbox is in memory only: a restart forgets it, on purpose.</li>
			</ul>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="Tested">
				<p>
					The server's tests send mail from a request and from a job, as text and as HTML, to an SMTP server
					the test runs; to one that isn't there (an error, not a crash); and to the mailbox, its pages,
					its stream and its JSON, under <code>--dev</code> and <code>--debug</code> and neither, forgotten by
					a restart. The old flags (<code>--smtp-url</code>, <code>--mail-from</code>) are refused, saying where
					they went.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
